    "classic_presentation": "ONSET: Sudden or gradual worsening over hours to days. LOCATION: Diffuse chest tightness, bilateral. DURATION: Episodes lasting minutes to hours, can be persistent. CHARACTER: Chest tightness, pressure, 'can't get air in'. AGGRAVATING: Triggers (allergens, exercise, cold air, stress, infections). RELIEVING: Bronchodilators (albuterol), sitting upright, pursed-lip breathing. TIMING: Can be episodic or persistent. SEVERITY: Variable, can be mild to life-threatening. ASSOCIATED SYMPTOMS: Wheezing (audible or auscultated), cough (dry or productive), accessory muscle use, tripod positioning, inability to speak in full sentences, cyanosis (severe cases). KEY POSITIVES: Wheezing, chest tightness, dyspnea, cough, accessory muscle use. KEY NEGATIVES: No fever (unless infection trigger), no chest pain (unless severe).",
